✅ 2. Start Multiple Threads and Observe Scheduling

🔴 Thread Scheduling is the process by which the JVM decides which thread gets CPU time and when.
🔴 The JVM uses a Thread Scheduler (which relies on the underlying Operating System scheduler) to manage thread execution.

The scheduler determines:
🔴 Which thread runs first.
🔴 How long a thread runs.
🔴 When a thread is paused or resumed.

2.1 Why Do We Need Thread Scheduling?

Imagine three threads:

🔴 Download File
🔴 Send Email
🔴 Generate Report

Only one CPU core can execute one thread at a time (per core).

The scheduler decides:

CPU
 │
 ├── Download Thread
 ├── Email Thread
 └── Report Thread

Without scheduling:

🔴 Threads would compete for CPU indefinitely.
🔴 Some threads might never execute (starvation).

The system would become inefficient.

2.2 What is the Thread Scheduler?
🔴 The Thread Scheduler is responsible for selecting the next thread to execute.
🔴 Java does not define a specific scheduling algorithm.

Scheduling behavior depends on:
🔴 Operating System
🔴 JVM implementation
🔴 CPU availability
Thread priority (as a hint)

Important Interview Point:
Java does not guarantee thread execution order. The JVM delegates scheduling to the underlying operating system.

2.3 How Thread Scheduling Works

Suppose we create three threads:

Thread A
Thread B
Thread C

All call:

start();

Flow:

Create Threads
      │
      ▼
start()
      │
      ▼
Runnable State
      │
      ▼
Thread Scheduler
      │
 ┌────┼────┐
 ▼    ▼    ▼
 A    B    C

The scheduler decides which thread runs next.

6. Possible Outputs
Output 1
Thread-A : 1
Thread-A : 2
Thread-B : 1
Thread-C : 1
Thread-B : 2
...

Output 2
Thread-C : 1
Thread-C : 2
Thread-A : 1
Thread-B : 1
...


2.4 Why Does the Output Change Every Time?

Because:

🔴 Thread scheduling is non-deterministic.
🔴 The scheduler may switch between threads at any time.
🔴 CPU availability changes.
🔴 OS scheduling policies differ.

Multi-core processors allow true parallel execution.

2.5 What is Context Switching?
A Context Switch occurs when the CPU stops executing one thread and starts executing another.

During a context switch, the operating system saves the state (program counter, registers, stack, etc.)
of the current thread and restores the state of the next thread.